## Documenting and measuring hunts

An undocumented hunt cannot be repeated or defended. Record, for each hunt:

- the hypothesis and the ATT&CK technique;
- the data sources, time range and exact queries;
- the results: counts, the benign explanations you confirmed, and any escalations;
- the outcome: a new detection, a data gap to fix, or a clean negative result;
- the date it should be run again.

Useful program metrics are the number of hunts per quarter, detections created from hunts, data gaps found and closed, and ATT&CK techniques with verified coverage. Avoid counting "incidents found" alone. A mature environment may find few, and a program judged only on that number will be tempted to inflate its results.

### From hunt to detection

When a query is precise enough to run unattended, convert it. Add an allow list for the benign cases you found, set a threshold and a schedule, and assign an owner. The methods are covered in [Advanced Detection Engineering](/learn/security-operations-dfir/advanced-detection-engineering). A hunt that never becomes a detection solves the same problem every quarter.
